  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCLouds will be on the increase ahead of a warm frontal passage\nthis morning. Enough lift and moisture along the warm front may\nbe enough for some spotty showers further north along the NY\nThruway. Our main concern will be the cold frontal passage\ntonight. Instability looks to be a limiting factor for any\nstrong to severe thunderstorms but shear looks to be rather high\nfor this event. Hi-res modeling has a decent handle on the\ntiming from late afternoon into the overnight. Some of the\nconvective showers have the potential to bring down some gusty\nwinds or spin up a brief weak tornado even with little\nlightning. Also a few indications of localized rainfall of\naround an inch in an hours time. This may be sufficient enough\nfor some minor urban and small stream flooding issues. Highs\nshould get to around 80 with lows tonight in the 60's. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nWest to northwest flow comes in behind the front Thursday.\nModeled soundings do show the potential for some 20-25 mph wind\ngusts to be transported to the surface. The cooler airmass\nreally doesn't enter the region till Friday. Otherwise, still\nmore like late summer temperature wise. Temperatures should\nslowly warm through early next week with a few showers and\nthunderstorms around from time to time. NBM guidance is likely\ntoo cool with the temperatures given the anomalous warm air\nadvection and ridging by September standards. Would not be\nsurprised to see some locations get close to 90 by Tuesday. A\nstronger cold front with some shower and thunderstorm chances\nshould move into the area around that time.",
